<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s05e05" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 5: Square Roots</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 5 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Interpret square roots
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Calculate very simple square roots by hand. 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Calculate more complicated square roots on a calculator. 
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 5 Key Terminology</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Square root
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 5 Key Symbols</title>
    <p>
      <md>
        \sqrt{\quad}
      </md>
      <md>
        \text{as in}\,\, \sqrt{16}
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    Remember that squaring a number means raising it to a power of 2.
    <md>
      \text{square}\,\, 7 = 7^2 = 49
    </md>
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-square-root">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term> square root</term> is meant to undo or reverse the square.
        <md>
          <mrow>\text{square}\,\, 6 \amp = 36 </mrow>
          <mrow>\text{square root}\,\, 36 \amp =6</mrow>
        </md>
        The symbol for the square root is 
        <md>
          \sqrt{\quad}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    If we want to write something that means, “calculate the square root of 36,” we would write the 36 underneath the root symbol:
    <md>
      \sqrt{36} = 6
    </md>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Calculate the square root <m>\sqrt{100}</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Because <m>10^2 = 100</m>, we know:
      <md>
        \sqrt{100} = 10
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Calculate the square root <m>\sqrt{25}</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Because <m>5^2 = 25</m>, we know:
      <md>
        \sqrt{25} = 5
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Not all square roots work out to be whole numbers and should be calculated on a calculator:
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Calculate the square root below. Round to 2 decimal places if necessary.
      <md>
        \sqrt{7}
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      There's no nice whole number that becomes 7 after it's squared. So, we calculate this square root on a calculator:
      <md>
        <mrow> \sqrt{7} \amp = 2.64575131106…</mrow> 
        <mrow> \amp \approx 2.65 \,\,\text{after rounding}</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Notice Something Neat!</title>
    <p>
      The number 7 is between 4 and 9. So <m>\sqrt{7}</m> is between <m>\sqrt{4}=2</m> and <m>\sqrt{9}=3</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    The geometric object called a square is a special kind of rectangle whose length and width are equal:
  </p>
  <image width="75%">
    <shortdescription>A picture of a square with four equal sides</shortdescription>
    <prefigure label="pf-square-root-square" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
      <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="(75,20,75,20)">
        <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
        <rectangle lower-left="(-3,-3)" dimensions="(6,6)" stroke = "black" thickness="2"/>
        <label anchor="(0,3)" alignment="north">
        Side
        </label>
        <label anchor="(0,-3)" alignment="south">
        Side
        </label>
        <label anchor="(3,0)" alignment="east">
        Side
        </label>
        <label anchor="(-3,0)" alignment="west">
        Side
        </label>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
  <p>
    Use the variable <m>s</m> to represent the length and the width of the square. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Because of the rectangle area formula, the area of the square is the length, <m>s</m>, multiplied by the width, also <m>s</m>:
    <md>
      A = s\times s = s^2
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    So, squaring the side length gives us the square’s area. That means taking the square root of the area gives us the square’s side length:
    <md>
      s=\sqrt{A}
    </md>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      A square has an area of 10 square feet. Find the square’s side length. Round to 2 decimal places, if necessary.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: We can find the side length by taking the square root of the area:
      <md>
        <mrow>s \amp =\sqrt{10} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = 3.162277660168… </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp \approx 3.16 \,\,\text{feet}</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s05e05">
    <title>
    Season 5, Episode 5 Practice Problems
    </title>
    <p>
      Problem 1: Calculate the following square roots by hand:
    </p>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{49}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{1}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{0}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{121}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{81}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{4}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{16}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.9in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{36}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    
    <p>
      Calculate the following square roots using a calculator. Round to 3 decimal places, if necessary.
    </p>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{12}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{15}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{18}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{20}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{8}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{6}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{3}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{50}</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
      <p><m>\sqrt{27}</m></p>
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>
</section>  